Z||000003||Her86GT||05-22-2001||07:09 PM||||Unless you have lost a great deal of your compression from fuel wash.  Try removing the spark plugs and squirt in oil into each cylinder and crank over couple of times. Reinstall spark plugs and compression restored.  Fuel from a flooded or extensively cranked over engine can reduce compression.<p>[This message has been edited by Her86GT (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||HummelHund||05-22-2001||07:40 PM||||Check your ignition module? I don't know if it can cause your sort of problem in a Fiero, but it did in my '91 Mercury Capri. The car showed spark, fuel, and had good compression, but would not start. We replaced the distributor and ignition module (they were a single unit for the Capri) and it cranked right up.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
